-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('58f2c27b-5111-5491-9882-ceb303dad66f', 'rg11-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Hill on Mile Two$q$, $q$Everyone on the Brookfield cross-country team knew about the hill on mile two. Freshmen heard stories about it before their first practice: it was a mountain, it went straight up into the clouds, it had swallowed entire seasons of good intentions. By the time Priya Raman actually saw it, she was almost disappointed. It was steep, certainly, but it ended at a fence and a water tower, not at the edge of the sky.

Her coach, Ms. Adeyemi, had a strange rule for the hill. "If you want to get up it faster," she told the team, "slow down at the bottom."

The first time Priya ran the course in a race, she ignored the advice. She charged the slope as if she could outrun gravity, passing three girls in the first twenty meters. Halfway up, her legs turned to wet cardboard. All three girls passed her back, and one of them even said "good job" in a voice that was entirely too cheerful.

At the next meet, Priya tried it the coach's way. She shortened her stride, kept her arms low, and let the leaders pull away. It felt like losing on purpose. Near the top, though, she noticed that she still had breath left, while the runners ahead of her were laboring. She crested the hill beside the fence and passed four of them on the way down.

Afterward, Priya found Ms. Adeyemi near the finish chute. "I hated every step of that hill," she said, grinning.

"Good," said the coach. "That means you finally respected it."$q$, 'draft')
