  ('f198a448-7e70-59e7-8684-3d98f6278582', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The author praises "a map that confesses its limits." Why is "confesses" a more effective word here than "states"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Confesses\" and \"states\" carry identical feelings, so neither matters.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Confesses\" suggests the map is hiding its limits from the reader.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-opposite"},{"text":"\"Confesses\" suggests openly admitting a weakness, which feels honest.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Confesses\" implies that the mapmaker committed a crime and was caught.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Confesses" suggests openly admitting a weakness, which feels honest.$q$, $q$"Confesses" carries the sense of admitting something unflattering, which connects to the earlier idea of blank spaces as "honest." "States" is neutral, and nothing in the passage suggests wrongdoing, so the crime reading is too literal.$q$, 'draft', '785f8d37-79b3-5bda-9bc0-56a4f5a84eee')
