   $q$Researchers say an ice core lets them "read the atmosphere of the distant past." What does "read" mean in this context?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Listen to sounds recorded inside the trapped air bubbles","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Look at words that ancient people wrote about the weather","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Interpret evidence to learn what the past air was like","is_correct":true},{"text":"Predict what the atmosphere will be like in the future","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-past-future"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Interpret evidence to learn what the past air was like$q$, $q$The next sentence shows scientists "measuring the gases trapped in those bubbles" to estimate past carbon dioxide, which is interpreting evidence like reading a text. There are no written words in the ice, so the literal reading does not fit.$q$, 'draft', '749dcbcc-aaba-5b63-9630-c346625f1c23')
