  ('c6cfc528-32e2-528d-8a0f-45d0a291e87f', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author calls the study of ice "paradoxical" because ice is fragile "yet it has preserved information longer than almost any written document." How does the final paragraph build on this paradox?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It explains that scientists prefer cores that have already started to melt.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It shows that ice is actually stronger than writing, so no paradox exists.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misses-paradox"},{"text":"It suggests that written documents will soon replace ice cores in research.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows the record's survival depends on cold, so shrinking glaciers threaten it.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows the record's survival depends on cold, so shrinking glaciers threaten it.$q$, $q$The paradox ends with "Its permanence depends entirely on staying cold," and the final paragraph shows what happens when that condition fails: the archive is "disappearing." The paradox remains, because ice is lasting only while cold, not stronger in general.$q$, 'draft', '749dcbcc-aaba-5b63-9630-c346625f1c23')
