-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('936952f3-ff9f-55d2-b339-31906970cce9', 'rg11-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Draw Offer$q$, $q$The final round of the regional chess tournament had been going for three hours, and Amara Nwosu's board looked like a quiet field after a storm. Most of the pieces were gone. She had a king, a rook, and four pawns. Her opponent, a tall boy named Henrik Lindqvist, had the same.

The tournament hall was so silent that Amara could hear a clock ticking on a table across the room. It sounded, she thought, like someone tapping a pencil on the inside of her skull.

Henrik pushed a pawn forward, pressed his clock, and quietly offered a draw. Amara's teammates were watching from the doorway. A draw would give their school second place, a solid result. A win would give them first. A loss would drop them to fourth.

She studied the position. Her coach liked to say that in chess the boldest move is often to do nothing at all, and for the first time she thought she understood him. Henrik's pawns were slightly advanced, which looked strong but left them exposed. If she simply waited, shuffling her rook back and forth, he might have to move something he did not want to move.

Amara shook her head politely and declined. Then she made the most patient move of her life: she slid her rook one square to the left.

Twenty moves later, Henrik's advanced pawn fell, and the game turned. When he finally resigned, he shook her hand and said, "You beat me by not doing anything."

"I did plenty," Amara said, smiling. "I just did it slowly."$q$, 'draft')
