   $q$The ticking clock sounds "like someone tapping a pencil on the inside of her skull." What is the role of this comparison?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It explains that a spectator was tapping a pencil nearby.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It shows that Amara had a headache before the round started.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It suggests that the clock was broken and making strange noises.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"It shows how the silence made a small sound feel tense and intrusive.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows how the silence made a small sound feel tense and intrusive.$q$, $q$The hall is "so silent" that a distant clock seems to tap inside her head, conveying pressure and focus at a tense moment. No one is actually tapping a pencil; the comparison describes how the clock feels to her.$q$, 'draft', '936952f3-ff9f-55d2-b339-31906970cce9')
