   $q$Amara's coach says that "the boldest move is often to do nothing at all." What does this paradox mean in the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Patient waiting can take courage and force an opponent into trouble.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Bold players should always attack quickly to finish the game.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Players should skip their turns whenever the position is unclear.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Chess rewards luck more than planning, so effort is wasted.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Patient waiting can take courage and force an opponent into trouble.$q$, $q$Amara reasons that if she "simply waited," Henrik "might have to move something he did not want to move," and his pawn later falls. Turning down a safe draw to wait is risky, which is why waiting counts as bold.$q$, 'draft', '936952f3-ff9f-55d2-b339-31906970cce9')
