  ('dda88549-f17a-54f7-9bc8-459a546d214f', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The author writes that the internet "remembers what people wish it would forget and forgets what people need it to remember." What does this paradox express?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The internet stores everything forever, so nothing is ever truly lost.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Trivial or embarrassing posts linger, while important sources disappear.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Websites deliberately delete useful sources to protect people's privacy.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"People forget important sources because they spend too much time online.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Trivial or embarrassing posts linger, while important sources disappear.$q$, $q$The paragraph pairs "embarrassing photos" that resurface with forgotten "sources that scholars, journalists, and students depend on." Saying nothing is lost captures only the first half of the paradox and ignores the forgetting.$q$, 'draft', '2c939315-9602-56db-a4ae-170c85dba003')
